00:46:13.100 --> 00:47:01.150
<v Ira Glass>Act three, My Angel's in the Centerfold. There's a story that my mom used to tell about when my sisters and I were little. The Gosgoth's cat next door to us had kittens. My sister Randi was six or seven at the time, and she asked my mom, OK, so how exactly did that happen? Like what happened to make the cat pregnant? And my mom, she just thought like, oh, this is really great. She's asking me these questions. We can get this out of the way. She's so young. And so she tells Randi the whole story and reproduction, and the mommy and the daddy cat, the whole thing. She gets to the end of this whole story. My mom is feeling great. And she turns to Randi and she says, do you have any questions, Randi? And Randi thinks for a second and she says, can birds really fly?
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，折页里的天使。我妈妈过去常讲一个故事，关于我和姐妹们小时候的事。隔壁高斯格思家的猫生了小猫。我妹妹兰迪当时六七岁，她问我妈，“好的，那到底是怎么发生的？猫是怎么怀孕的？”我妈当时想，噢，这太好了。她在问我这些问题。我们可以把这事搞定。她还那么小。于是她给兰迪讲了关于繁殖的整个故事，猫妈妈和猫爸爸，整个过程。她讲完了整个故事。我妈感觉很棒。她转向兰迪说，“兰迪，你有什么问题吗？”兰迪想了一会儿说，“鸟真的会飞吗？”

00:47:01.150 --> 00:47:24.000
<v Ira Glass>I think the moral of the story is you can throw certain information at kids, but they're only going to absorb what they are able to absorb, and they're going to ignore everything else. And I bring this up here, because our next story is very much an example of that phenomenon. A warning to listeners, there's nothing explicit or graphic in this story, but it does acknowledge the existence of sex. Thea Chaloner tells the tale.
<v Ira Glass>我想这故事的寓意是你可以向孩子们灌输某些信息，但他们只会吸收他们能够吸收的，他们会忽略其他一切。我在这里提到这一点，是因为我们的下一个故事非常符合那种现象。对听众的警告，这个故事中没有什么露骨或生动的内容，但它确实承认了性的存在。西娅·查洛纳讲述了这个故事。

00:47:24.000 --> 00:47:43.170
<v Thea Chaloner>My friend Emily had a rough time growing up with her dad. Part of the problem was his job. He taught human sexuality at Boston University, which is pretty impressive. But to a kid, it's your worst nightmare. Your dad is a sex professor. By the time she was 10, he was a local celebrity, famous for his knowledge about sex.
<v Thea Chaloner>我的朋友艾米丽在和她爸爸成长的过程中度过了一段艰难的时期。问题的一部分是他的工作。他在波士顿大学教人类性学，这很令人印象深刻。但对一个孩子来说，这是你最糟糕的噩梦。你爸爸是个性学教授。到她10岁时，他已经是当地名人，以他的性知识闻名。

00:47:43.170 --> 00:48:29.100
<v Emily Helfgot>He had a radio show on WHDH in Boston called Talking Sex with Doctor Joseph Helfgot . It was a call-in show. And it was a maverick show of its time because he would talk frankly about sex. Most of the people who called in were women talking about how-- I mean, I can clearly remember, I can hear a woman's voice calling in and asking if he could explain to her how she could achieve multiple orgasm. So at Rebecca Ritter's house, fifth grade, in my Care Bear nightgown, in my Strawberry Shortcake sleeping bag, I swear to God, as like girly as it gets. And then we are in our sleeping bags about to go to sleep, and Rebecca sits up and says, let's listen to Em's dad's show.
<v Emily Helfgot>他在波士顿的WHDH有一个广播节目，叫《与约瑟夫·赫尔夫戈特博士谈性》。这是一个观众打进电话的节目。那是当时一个特立独行的节目，因为他会坦率地谈论性。大多数打电话进来的都是女性，谈论如何……我的意思是，我可以清楚地记得，我能听到一个女人的声音打进来，问他能否向她解释如何才能达到多重高潮。所以当时在丽贝卡·里特家，五年级，我穿着我的“关心熊”睡衣，钻在我的“草莓短饼”睡袋里，我发誓，真的不能再女孩气了。然后我们在睡袋里准备睡觉，丽贝卡坐起来说：“听听艾姆爸爸的节目吧。”

00:48:29.100 --> 00:49:19.940
<v Emily Helfgot>So they turn the radio on and we'd be like in the dark, listening to my father on the radio going, [EXHALES]-- because he also smoked-- Doris, I'm really glad you called in. Women so often think that it's something that's going on with them, that's the reason why they can't achieve multiple orgasm. And he was very flirtatious with the people on the air, too. And I knew that, I could sense the tenor of the flirtation, even when I was a little kid. And I was trying to pass it off and be giggly and like, oh, it's so funny, like all my friends were. But I was putting my nails into my thigh to get myself not to cry. And I also just felt, why couldn't my dad be normal?
<v Emily Helfgot>所以他们打开收音机，我们就在黑暗中，听我父亲在收音机里说——（呼气）——因为他也抽烟——“多丽丝，我很高兴你打来电话。女性通常认为那是她们自己的原因导致无法达到多重高潮。”他对节目里的那些人也很调情。我知道那点，即使我还是个小孩子，我也能感觉到那种调情的语气。我试图把它掩盖过去，笑嘻嘻地，像我所有朋友一样。但我把指甲掐进大腿里，让自己别哭出来。我也只是觉得，为什么我爸爸就不能正常一点呢？

00:49:19.940 --> 00:49:43.990
<v Thea Chaloner>Emily's parents divorced when she was five, and she and her brother spent every other weekend at her dad's house. He enforced a lot of family time, which usually meant watching TV. Then he'd fall asleep and the kids were left to amuse themselves. Her brother occupied himself with Archie comics and GI Joes But Emily was on her own. It was weird being there, and boring, until she discovered the bookshelves.
<v Thea Chaloner>艾米丽的父母在她五岁时离婚，她和哥哥每隔一个周末就去她爸爸家。他强制要求很多家庭时间，通常就是看电视。然后他会睡着，孩子们就只能自己找乐子。她哥哥摆弄阿奇漫画和特种部队人偶。但艾米丽只能靠自己。在那里感觉很怪，很无聊，直到她发现了书架。

00:49:43.990 --> 00:50:31.670
<v Emily Helfgot>It was all books on the top three bookshelves, and then the bottom bookshelf was all Playboys . I mean, it was like a 64 Crayola box of Playboys . And my first mission, because I was always a very kind of organizationally oriented kid, was to chronologically organize them. So I had them going October '81, or whatever it was, all the way through to whatever the current one was. Somehow-- and I wish I could tell you how I figured this out-- I decided that I would use the magazines, and kind of use the centerfolds and the questionnaires that they would answer, as a portfolio for women who I would use in my dating service.
<v Emily Helfgot>书架顶上三层全是书，然后最下面一层全是《花花公子》。我的意思是，那就像一盒64色的《花花公子》蜡笔。我的第一个任务，因为我一直是一个非常有组织倾向的孩子，就是按年代整理它们。所以我把它们从81年10月，或者不管是什么时候，一直排到最新的一本。不知怎么的——我真希望能告诉你我是怎么想出来的——我决定我将使用这些杂志，把折页和她们回答的问卷作为我婚介服务中女性的个人资料。

00:50:31.670 --> 00:50:44.150
<v Thea Chaloner>The tools for her imaginary dating service were: a Smith Corona typewriter, a phone unplugged from the wall, a box of index cards, a Yellow Pages, and of course, a huge collection of Playboys .
<v Thea Chaloner>她虚构的婚介服务的工具是：一台史密斯-科罗纳打字机、一部从墙上拔掉插头的电话、一盒索引卡、一本黄页，当然，还有一大堆《花花公子》杂志。

00:50:44.150 --> 00:50:48.610
<v Thea Chaloner>Take me through a couple of sample scenarios of what this would be. Can you do that?
<v Thea Chaloner>带我看看这些情况的一些样本场景。你能做到吗？

00:50:48.610 --> 00:51:34.690
<v Emily Helfgot>I can, but I should, as the disclaimer, first say that I would say my lines out loud, but I wouldn't say the lines of the quote unquote, "person on the other line of the phone." [PHONE RING] Miss Lana's Dating Service, how can I help you? Uh-huh. So you're looking for someone who's like 5'10", and weighs 125 pounds? Can you hold on for just a minute, sir? And then I would press-- actually, I don't believe there was a hold button, but in my imagination there was, and I would put the phone down. And I would go to my resource library of Playboy magazines, and I would go until I found someone that was around the range that I had just made up for myself. So really it's very twisted.
<v Emily Helfgot>我可以，但我应该先声明一下，我会大声说出我的台词，但我不会说出“电话那头的人”的台词。（电话铃响）“兰娜小姐婚介服务，有什么能帮您的？”“嗯哼。所以你在找一个大约5英尺10英寸、体重125磅的人？您能稍微等一下吗，先生？”然后我会按——其实我不认为有一个保持通话按钮，但在我的想象中是有，我会放下电话。我会去我的《花花公子》杂志资源库，一直翻到找到一个符合我刚刚为自己编造的范围的人。所以这真的很扭曲。

00:51:34.690 --> 00:52:24.420
<v Emily Helfgot>And then I would open up to the centerfolds, and not really extend the whole picture of the centerfold, but really just looking at the questionnaire. And once I had that in front of me, I'd get back on the phone. Hi, Mr. Watkins? Yes, I think I have the perfect girl for you. Her name is Amber Rose. She is 5'9", 125 pounds, and she-- what? Yes, she does. She likes oysters, and she likes long walks on the beach at night. And oh, she loves shooting stars. Does that sound good? OK, Mr. Watkins, let me just get your information. And then I would put an index card in the typewriter, and roll it in. It was one of those old manual kind.
<v Emily Helfgot>然后我会翻开折页，并没有真正展开整张图片，只是真正看着调查问卷。一旦我面前有了那个，我就回到电话上。“喂，沃特金斯先生？是的，我想我有一个完美的女孩给你。她叫安布尔·罗斯。她5英尺9英寸，125磅，她——什么？是的，她确实。她喜欢牡蛎，她喜欢晚上在海滩上散步。噢，她喜欢流星。那听起来不错吗？好的，沃特金斯先生，我只要获取一下你的信息。”然后我会把一张索引卡放进打字机里，卷进去。那是那种老式的机械打字机。

00:52:24.420 --> 00:53:01.220
<v Emily Helfgot>And I would say, so it's Ron Watkins. And it would take me hunting and pecking on the typewriter, like Ron Watkins, spell it out, return, next line. And then I would type in the name of the centerfold. I've typed in the magazine she was from, like the month and the year so I'd have it for reference later. And then I would chat sometimes with him on the phone, like, so Mr. Watkins, how long have you been living in Washington? Uh-huh. Oh, you got a recommendation from-- oh, yeah, Bill comes to me all the time.
<v Emily Helfgot>我会说：“所以是罗恩·沃特金斯。”我会用打字机边找边按，像这样：“罗恩·沃特金斯”，拼出来，回车，下一行。然后我会打入折页女孩的名字。我打进她所属的杂志，比如月份和年份，这样我以后可以查阅。有时我还会和他聊天，“所以沃特金斯先生，你在华盛顿住多久了？嗯哼。噢，你是得到推荐的……噢，是的，比尔经常来找我。”

00:53:01.220 --> 00:53:10.860
<v Emily Helfgot>And I was a facilitator of the woman and the man getting together. I wasn't playing out any kind of fantasy for myself. I was a businesswoman.
<v Emily Helfgot>我是促成男女相见的中间人。我没有在为自己演绎任何幻想。我是一个女商人。

00:53:10.860 --> 00:53:22.750
<v Thea Chaloner>Except for the fact that the women are totally naked, in very sexual positions all throughout these magazines. So did that factor into your brain?
<v Thea Chaloner>除了一个事实，那就是这些杂志里到处都是裸体女性，姿势非常色情。这有进入你的大脑吗？

00:53:22.750 --> 00:53:54.590
<v Emily Helfgot>No, that's why I say that I really-- I mean, I would look at the actual pictures, really just so I could get a sense, because sometimes they were like, cowgirl. Like she would have no clothes on except for she'd have a bullwhip in her hand, and little spurs on her ankles, and she'd be lying on a bed of hay or something. So I would factor that into my conversation with the guy. I'd be like, and she loves to rodeo.
<v Emily Helfgot>不，这就是为什么我说我真的……我的意思是，我会看那些真实的图片，真的只是为了有个概念，因为有时她们像是在骑马。比如她没穿衣服，除了手里拿着一根牛鞭，脚踝上戴着小马刺，躺在一堆干草之类的东西上。所以我会在和男人的谈话中考虑这一点。我会说：“而且她热爱骑术。”

00:53:54.590 --> 00:54:22.420
<v Emily Helfgot>So then I took it to this other level. This is where the Yellow Pages comes in. Where I would say, so Ron, what kind of cuisine do you like? And I would find a place, and then I would type up on the index card the name of the restaurant, the time the reservation was going to be, and I filed the card away, I'm sure alphabetically, by the guy's last name in the little plastic case. And then it was time for the next call.
<v Emily Helfgot>所以后来我把它提升到了另一个层次。这就是黄页发挥作用的地方。我会说：“所以罗恩，你喜欢什么口味的美食？”我会找一家店，然后把餐厅名称、预订时间打在索引卡上，我肯定把卡片按字母顺序放在了那个小塑料盒里。然后就是下一个电话的时间了。

00:54:22.420 --> 00:54:49.530
<v Emily Helfgot>I could not have been more content. I always wanted to be a travel agent as a kid, you know the clicking, clicking of the nails on the keyboard, and the simultaneously talking to-- and this was before I think they even had headset phones. So the simultaneous talking to the person, I think I have a good deal for you, and then like referencing things in books, just using all those tools. And the resources available to me were Playboy magazines.
<v Emily Helfgot>我当时非常满足。我小时候一直想当旅行社职员，你知道敲击键盘的咔嗒声，还有同时和人交谈——这在我认为他们甚至有耳机电话之前。所以同时和人交谈，“我想我有一个好交易给你”，然后从书里找东西，只是使用所有那些工具。我能利用的资源就是《花花公子》杂志。

00:54:49.530 --> 00:55:04.350
<v Emily Helfgot>It was also just a coping mechanism for being in an environment that I hated. I didn't like being there for so many reasons, and it was an escape.
<v Emily Helfgot>这同样是一种应对机制，为了应付一个我讨厌的环境。我讨厌在那里待着的原因有很多，这是一种逃避。

00:55:04.350 --> 00:55:32.880
<v Thea Chaloner>For Emily, the long term effects of looking at so many Playboys at such a young age weren't as damaging as you'd expect. When she was 10, she believed that she could choose the centerfold body she liked the best, and then she'd grow into it later on, like that was an option. What was damaging was that her dad just didn't seem to get that she was still a kid and that certain things were better left unsaid. Or in the case of the Playboys , certain things are better left stashed away on that top shelf of the closet.
<v Thea Chaloner>对艾米丽来说，在这么小的年纪看这么多《花花公子》的长期影响并不像你想象的那么具破坏性。10岁时，她相信她可以挑选她最喜欢的折页身材，然后以后长成那样，就像那是个选项一样。具破坏性的是，她爸爸似乎不明白她还是个孩子，有些事情最好不要说。或者在《花花公子》的情况下，有些事情最好收在壁橱的最上面那一层。

00:55:32.880 --> 00:56:33.570
<v Emily Helfgot>A 10 year old, 11 year old, 12 year old girl is not impressed by her father's call in radio sex show. She's not impressed by the fact that he can talk so openly about sex, because as far as she's concerned, that conversation doesn't exist between the father and the daughter. I think what you have to understand is that to cope as a kid, you read a situation when you walk into it, and you conform to the situation. And being able to enter into this-- especially from the world of my mother's house, that was like everything was in its right place, and we got tucked in. And I got my back rubbed in a very certain way. And the door left open just three inches, and then good night, sweet dreams, I love you, with the glass of water. And it's like, we're going to dad's, a different routine. I can fight it and be miserable or I can kind of make a game up with the Playboys until we get back to my mom's house.
<v Emily Helfgot>一个10岁、11岁、12岁的女孩不会对她爸爸的电台性节目印象深刻。她不会因为他能如此公开地谈论性而留下印象，因为据她看来，那种对话不存在于父女之间。我认为你必须明白的是，作为孩子为了应对，当你走进一个环境时，你会解读它，并顺应它。能够进入这个——尤其是从我妈妈房子的世界走出来，那里一切都井然有序，我们被塞进被子里。我的背会以一种特定的方式被抚摸。门只留开三英寸，然后晚安，甜蜜的梦，我爱你，还有那杯水。这感觉就是，“我们要去爸爸那了，换个流程。”我可以反抗并变得悲惨，或者我可以一边玩《花花公子》的游戏，一边等我们回到我妈妈家。

00:56:33.570 --> 00:56:37.000
<v Thea Chaloner>Well, it's like, I can fight it, or I can alphabetize.
<v Thea Chaloner>嗯，就像是，“我可以反抗，或者我可以按字母排序。”

00:56:37.000 --> 00:56:42.000
<v Emily Helfgot>Yes, exactly.
<v Emily Helfgot>是的，完全正确。

00:56:42.000 --> 00:57:12.470
<v Thea Chaloner>Emily lost her virginity when she was 17. She told both parents. Her mom's first response was sweet: do you love him? Were you safe? Her dad's? Was it good? Did you orgasm? And yes, at the time, her dad's question completely freaked her out. But now she thinks his intentions were the same as her mom's. His heart was in the right place. He just didn't understand how to talk to his daughter. It's the oldest dad problem in the world.
<v Thea Chaloner>艾米丽在17岁时失去了童贞。她告诉了父母双方。她妈妈的第一反应很甜：“你爱他吗？你做好安全措施了吗？”她爸爸呢？“那感觉好吗？你达到高潮了吗？”是的，当时她爸爸的问题彻底让她崩溃了。但现在她认为他的意图和她妈妈的一样。他的心在正确的地方。他只是不明白如何和女儿沟通。这是世界上最古老的父亲问题。

00:57:12.470 --> 00:57:20.790
<v Ira Glass>Thea Chaloner. She was, until last week, our intern here at This American Life . She's moving back to Los Angeles to become a producer at public radio station KCRW.
<v Ira Glass>西娅·查洛纳。直到上周，她还是我们《美国生活》的实习生。她要搬回洛杉矶，去公共广播电台KCRW担任制作人。

